            <ArticleTitle>Morbidly Obese vs Nonobese Aseptic Revision Total Hip Arthroplasty: Surprisingly Similar Outcomes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>842-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arth.2015.08.036</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0883-5403(15)00790-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We aimed to compare the outcomes between matched morbidly obese (BMI ≥ 40 kg/m(2)) and nonobese (BMI &lt;30 kg/m(2)) patients undergoing first-time aseptic revision THA with at least 4 years of follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Groups were matched 1:1 using sex, age, and date of revision surgery (123 patients in each group).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The overall incidence and risk of complication, reoperation, and re-revision were similar between groups. Morbidly obese patients were more likely to dislocate (odds ratio [OR], 3.3; P = .03), but were less likely to develop polyethylene wear (OR, 0.1; P = .04) and aseptic loosening (OR, 0.3; P = .03).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Quality outcome measures such as hospital readmission were not addressed by this study and could be the basis for future studies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">level III, prognostic study.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
